    },
    "How a NULL is set apart from a real value. Differentiate Without Color keeps the italic face whatever this says." : {

    },
    "How far apart two statements are left after a semicolon." : {

    },
    "How long a connect attempt may take before it is reported as a failure. An sslmode=prefer connection spends three fifths of this trying TLS and the rest retrying without it." : {

    },
    "How long a connection may be quiet before the server sends its first keepalive probe. 0 uses the server's own value." : {

    },
    "How long a message at the foot of the window stays. A few messages ask for longer on their own and keep it." : {

    },
    "How long an unused connection is kept in the pool before it is closed. 0 keeps them for as long as the maximum lifetime allows." : {

    },
    "How long the server lets a transaction of yours sit open and idle before it ends the session. It stops a forgotten transaction from holding locks. 0 turns it off." : {

    },
    "How long the server waits between probes that go unanswered. 0 uses the server's own value." : {

    },
    "How many of the newest entries the Results History navigator fetches. The list is one fetch, not pages, so this is all of the history you can see at once. Nothing is deleted: a lower number only shows fewer." : {

    },
    "How many rows an export fetches per round trip. A larger batch is fewer round trips and more memory at once. 5000 is what every export used before this was a setting." : {

    },
    "How many server connections one open database may use at once. More lets queries, metadata and exports run side by side; fewer is kinder to a server with a low connection limit." : {

    },
    "How many tag colours the bar at the left edge of a row may show. A row with more tags is not hiding them: the tooltip and the Inspector always list every one." : {

    },
    "How many unanswered probes end the connection. 0 uses the server's own value." : {

    },
    "How much the database engine writes to the system log. Warnings and errors is what Pharos has always written; the louder levels are for working out why a connection or a query misbehaves, and they slow nothing down when nobody is reading. Setting RUST_LOG in the shell that starts Pharos overrides this." : {

    },
    "How NULL renders in the grid and the Inspector." : {

    },
    "How often the open tabs are written down. Off still saves at quit, so turning it off does not lose the session." : {

    },
    "How old a connection's cached schema may be before Pharos fetches it again. 0 keeps it until the connection closes, which is what Pharos has always done." : {

    },
    "How strongly a matched cell is washed with its tag's colour. The wash always stays lighter than the find match you are looking at, so the two can be told apart." : {

    },
    "How the find field matches a cell. An expression that cannot be read turns the field red and matches nothing." : {

